**Model (no figure is printed, so the set-up is described here).** The hinge is at $A$, on the vertical wall; the rod $AB$ is horizontal and points away from the wall, with $AB = 4a$, so $AC = 3a$. $D$ is on the wall directly above $A$. The string $CD$ makes $60^\circ$ with the wall, that is with the vertical. Forces on the rod:

- weight of the rod $2mg$ downwards, at its midpoint ($2a$ from $A$, the rod being uniform);
- weight of the load $mg$ downwards, at $B$ ($4a$ from $A$);
- tension $T$ in the string at $C$, directed up and towards the wall: vertical component $T\cos 60^\circ$ upwards, horizontal component $T\sin 60^\circ$ towards the wall;
- reaction at the hinge $A$, with horizontal component $H$ and vertical component $V$ (their senses are found in part (b)).

Take moments about $A$ (this removes $H$ and $V$). The horizontal component of $T$ acts along the line of the rod, so it has no moment about $A$. The weights tend to turn the rod downwards and the vertical component of $T$ upwards:

$$T\cos 60^\circ \times 3a = 2mg \times 2a + mg \times 4a$$

$$\frac{3a}{2}\,T = 8mga \;\Rightarrow\; T = \frac{16}{3}\,mg.\qquad\blacksquare$$
